<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dmytro Vyshnevetsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1516–<a href="/years/1563">1563</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Baida</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dmytro Vyshnevetsky, remembered in Ukrainian folk song as Baida, was a Ruthenian prince and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> leader. Around 1552 he built a fort on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mala-khortytsia">Mala Khortytsia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, often regarded as the forerunner of the Zaporozhian Sich, and led campaigns against the Crimean Khanate and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Captured during an expedition to Moldavia, he was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1563.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dmytro-vyshnevetsky.jpg" alt="Dmytro Vyshnevetsky" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dmytro_%27Baida%27_Vyshnevetsky_(Portrait,_18th_century,_National_Museum_of_the_History_of_Ukraine).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
